#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""xuuxij-bonsai - grows bonsai trees in the terminal until a key is pressed.

Each tree is planned first, in one of the classic forms drawn by weight: the informal
upright (moyogi), the formal upright (chokkan), the slanting tree (shakan), the literati
(bunjin) and the cascade (kengai), a fifth of the uprights with a twin trunk. The trunk
is a spline with movements that tighten toward the top, tapering from a wide base with
surface roots; a few main branches leave it in turn, each ending in a cloud of foliage,
a back pad gives depth, and the whole sits off-centre in an ornate planter matched to
the form (after the pots of the Omiya Bonsai Art Museum and the classic Chinese and
Japanese containers), the composition centred on the screen. Then it is drawn like pen
strokes, bottom up, one cell after another with a bright moving tip, the leaves opening
as buds. The finished tree holds for a while; then its leaves turn, let go in the breeze
and float down to the soil; the bare tree is held, then crumbles into the planter; and a
new one grows out of the same planter, over the carpet the last one left, which piles up
a little more with every autumn. Only a key clears it, by ending the screensaver.

Settings live in ~/.config/xuuxij-bonsai.conf, one "key = value" per line. The file
is read again before every tree, so an edit shows on the next tree. A flag on the
command line wins over the file. --help lists the settings; --print-config shows
what a run would use.
"""

